 took icons they made icons which  looked like things that you would see in  an office and created applications  behind this so you can see things from a  an office at that time you know paper  filing cabinets manila folders  in and out trays and pocket calculators  and this has been a really durable  metaphor you can see 40 years into the  future the iPhone if you squint a little  bit it kind of looks like it's you know  basically the same thing you've got a  row of icons with pictures on the front  describing what they do and what I  really like about this in particular is  if you see their their calculator icon  from the Xerox star doesn't even really  look out of place on an iPhone from  today another durable metaphor is that  of the teletype the teletype was an  electromechanical device it came around  before mainframes it was used for  sending telegrams but when mainframes  were being developed they repurposed  this teletype to send and receive  messages to and from the mainframe and  this teletype is what we use today or  what we are emulating today when we use  a command line it's a remarkably durable  abstraction because it has proved so  useful and so these two things the  command line in the disk top are two  different ways of thinking about the  computer and interacting with it and  they change the way that you think about  computing each of them have distinct  benefits and they open up different  possibilities and constrain others and  computing has its own set of myths this  is the jargon file and who's read this  before seen this pretty much everyone so  this is written by Eric Raymond who also  wrote the cathedral and the bazaar and  in it he talks about this what I'm gonna  call it a myth of the real UNIX hecka  and this person is someone who writes  and C they do system software they use  the command line you know they are close  to the middle and this is not wrong you  know they're certainly people like this  I had breakfast with one really nice  person this morning who does this kind  of stuff and that's great but I think  this does myth of the UNIX Hecker as  being a true this is the true programmer  has been incredibly powerful incredibly  long-lasting in  incredibly damaging and it's constrained  the way we think about computing and who  can be a programmer and what programming  is an open source is full of its own  metaphors to one of the most dominant  metaphors as that of competition and  ever since I've been aware of open  source software it's always been in  competition with closed source software  there's a competition of you know is  open source gonna win as closed source  gonna win and even within programming  languages you know is it Ruby or Python  or closure or Java you know there's a  constant tension and rivalry between  programming languages and then even  within a programming community we have  rivalries and tensions and there's this  assumption deeply baked assumption that  growth is good that user growth is the  most important thing for a programming  language community or a technical  community in general in the way we use  this language influences how we and  others think about open source this is a  a Commons and England and the Commons  you may have heard of the tragedy of the  Commons it's not always so clear what  the Commons actually was and the Commons  was a real thing it was common land  owned by one person but which another  set of people had rights over and so  they were allowed to graze their sheep  or clicked firewood or fish on it or  different they had different sets of  rights and it was common land  collectively owned or collectively able  to be used but owned by someone else and  so there was a theory economic theory  that the Commons was unsustainable that  it wasn't possible to have this shared  resource where people were going to  overuse it and destroy it for others if  there wasn't a private control over that  and so I guess there's two problems with  that firstly this was you know pretty  thoroughly debunked by Elinor Ostrom  who got a Nobel Prize for her work on  you know common public goods and how  they can be shared and can work as a  public resource and secondly this kind  of metaphor presupposes that we have a  scarce good and when you apply this to  open source software  it really falls down because open-source  software has no imaginal costs of  distribution if one of you takes you  know a download of nginx and starts  using it it doesn't take away anything  from anybody else you can't overuse  engine x2 you take it to damage anybody  else's ability to use engine X another  metaphor this one I really do like is  that of infrastructure and you may have  read Nadia Igbos work on roads and  bridges which is about the critical  infrastructure underpinning large parts  of society which is often open source  work and often underappreciated and the  funded ends not necessarily maintained  very well and so I think this her work  has brought forward people's attention  and understanding of the need for us to  be maintaining open source software over  time and one of the ways that people  will think about funding open source  software is through donations and well I  think you know it's great when people do  get donations  I think donations can be the wrong way  to frame things donations typically our  thought of as charity they thought of as  providing value to somebody else but  they're not we're not doing it for  ourselves we're not getting anything in  return we are just giving the money  because it's a good thing to do which is  great but I think if we do this we miss  some of the some of the benefit of open  source in some of the reasons why we  would want to fund it it's not just  because it's a good thing to do but if  you're using this open source you  personally add deriving value from it as  well another metaphor for open source is  patronage and patronage was much more  common about you know four or five  hundred years ago in Renaissance times  where you if you were an artist or a  musician you probably couldn't make  money selling your artwork or music  directly and what would happen is that  there would be rich patrons who would  sponsor you 14 or 20 years perhaps and  they would pay for your materials they  would give you a place to live they  would give you a stipend to to work  and in exchange you would create your  art for them your creators buy them  directly for them or you would create it  and it would be you know knowing that  they were the ones who had sponsored  this work and this was great know we got  you know many many beautiful pieces of  artwork and music and all sorts of  things created through this but there  are some downsides to the patronage  relationship this is a painting of the  School of Athens by Raphael who was who  had the Medici family was his patrons  and this is many scholars this is a  painting of ancient scholars and  philosophers arguing and debating and in  a temple and what's really interesting  at this is this person here is not a  scholar or a philosopher this is the  Pope at the times nephew and the Duke of  Urbino and he was drawn in there not  because he was a you know famed scholar  or a famed mind he wasn't even from the  right generation you know he was you  know fifteen hundred years too recent to  fit him with the rest of these people  but because of this because of this  influence from the patrons he was he was  drawn in there either to curry favor  with his patrons or perhaps he was even  asked to be drawn in there and the first  thing you might think about anything of  patronage and open source is patreon and  patron yes uses some of the same  language but I think a much better  analogy as there of corporate  sponsorship of open source developers  where often companies will hire the  maintainer zyv open source projects and  let them just kind of work on it on  company time for half time or full time  perhaps and this is great