 so this is this is  fundamentally a generous act to share  your software with the world and I think  it's special I think it's something we  need to protect and we need to be a  little bit careful about when we talk  about funding open source and open  source sustainability I think there's a  risk that if we that we need to be  careful about how we do that so we don't  lose this you know this nature of open  source open source has its own set of  interfaces that we work with and these  are going to be taken from github  because github is largely the dominant  creative place where open source was  created and used  and contributed to first one here is the  issue tracker and we can see on the left  that github issue tracker and on the  right we have the JIRA issue tracker and  you can see straight away there's quite  a difference between the two on the Left  we have a title and an empty text field  and on the right in the JIRA issue  tracker there's ten fields there and we  still even haven't even got to typing  about the actual problem we have and  these are design decisions that's the  creators of this software have made and  they've done so and they put different  burdens of work on the creators of the  open-source versus their people who are  posting these issues and github you can  type just about whatever you like into  these text fields click Submit and now  it's on the maintainer plate they need  to look at it they need to figure out is  this a problem as this actionable can I  do anything about this did they even  post this to the right project and with  it where the JIRA side you've got a lot  more work needing that you need to do at  front before you can even you know click  the submit button in either of these are  necessarily wrong but the design  decisions we make building these  platforms change the way that people  interact with it and so when you're  building systems I think it's really  important to think about you know what  kind of patterns of behavior are we  encouraging or discouraging another way  another interface and github is this  contribution streaks the contribution  graph at the top is still there now but  the contribution streaks are gone and  this was done with the greatest of  intentions I believe but the idea was  that every day that you did work on open  source you would get a little green mark  on the graph and every day that you you  know consecutively worked on open source  including on the weekends you would  collect you build the streak up longer  and longer and so we can say this person  has worked for 177 days in a row on open  source work doing commits or opening  issues or PRS and what what we found or  what people started to realize was that  this was unsustainable  that they were staying to burn out they  were staying to feel really tired and  they couldn't go on but there were you  know pressures you know this is just you  know a silly little number  but yes you know it had had real effect  had an effect on me I remember doing  work on Saturdays and Sundays just so I  could keep that streak going  and ultimately github removed us they  realized I think that it was probably  doing more harm than good  and so we need to think a little bit  about the the platforms that we built  and Marshall McLuhan wrote this phrase  you've probably heard that before the  medium is the message and what it means  is that what is communicated is not just  the content delivered over the medium  but also the characteristics of the  medium itself the the framing that  github gives to the issue tracker  they're their way that they track your  contributions this is just as much part  of the message of open source as the  software being delivered through that  platform and this is why I'm really  excited to see github working on github  sponsors this is a new project that lets  you add anybody to support a project  into the github interface so you can see  there right next to the watching and  star buttons there is a button for  supporting a project and when you click  on it you see this you know dialog  pop-up and it shows you different ways  that you can support this project and  I'm really excited that github the  medium is sending this message that  funding open source is important and  when we created closures together we  adopted the metaphor of a grant making  organization this was a neutral  organization we didn't want to be  picking winners in the closure community  we wanted to you know spread the money  evenly where it was most needed and one  of the very first things we ran into was  an issue with how we communicated the  work that we were doing and we we  started off by saying we were going to  pay people 150 dollars an hour for 12  hours a month and this was going to work  out to $1,800 and the reason we chose  $150 an hour was firstly because we were  copying ruby together who came  for us and it seemed like a good idea  but also I really like the idea of  sending this message that encoded in  this 150 dollars an hour was the message  we're sending was this work is valuable  this is top tier software development  works the work that you are doing is an  open source developer is creating you  know value for thousands or tens of  thousands of people in the closure  community and we want to you know  respect that we can't perhaps offer you  a full-time salary but we want to say  that you know when you're working this  is what we think you're worth  unfortunately people perhaps didn't get  their message and they started saying  things like you're paying people too  much this is you know - you're you're  being too generous to open-source  project maintainer x' and they would say  things like you know you should pay them  you know I would do the work for $50 an  hour instead of one hundred and fifty  dollars an hour and this wasn't really  you know what we were going for we were  and we were funding the project  maintainer so we were finding the people  who were best suited to be doing this  work so we went back to the drawing  board and thought a little bit more  about this and we came up with a more  straightforward grant mechanism where we  would just pay the maintainer $1,800  each month and there was no hourly rate  attached there was no fixed number of  hours and this was fine it was exactly  the same amount of money in both cases  but the way that we had framed this you  know changed how people saw what we were  doing and when we're designing  open-source funding systems or  interacting with open-source maintained  as we need to think about the way that  we communicate the value that we are  doing and so this talk has been talking  about funding open source and I've been  doing a lot of talk about the language  we use and the reason why is that the  language we use leads to different  feelings and perceptions and beliefs  about open source software and that  leads to what we think about which leads  to the decisions we make any actions  that we take to fund open source  software and so if we just start at the  bottom and say you know there's a  problem with the open source software  not being funded enough and we don't  attack it at the root of the problem  then I'm not sure you know if we're  gonna be able to you know really address  the issue and so  I think you know we need to think about  the whole system not just the part at  the end and if we believe that when we  need to design systems that reflect the  fact that open source is free but it's  not worthless and design systems that  recognize the value and the work being  provided and in the work that these  maintained errs are doing and so I'd  like to propose a new metaphor for open  source work which is that of partnership  partnership between the creators of open  source work and the users of open source  that it's an exchange of value that open  source users may be will contribute time  or money or attention or other things of  value that they have towards the open  source project and the open source  project in return creates software that  is valuable for them and a partnership  is generative it's something which  creates more value than you put in the  the and this is true of all good  partnerships and I think all good  partnerships can't are not transactional  either this is not I'm not proposing  that this becomes like a sign up system  where you you know to use open source  you have to like put down your name as I  am a partner of this open source project  I think it should be freely given I  think this is this is an opportunity if  you want to you can help support the  open source software that you work with  and see see it grow and see it gets  tremendously better but I don't think it  should be forced I think that this  should still be the ability to use open  source software without without this  forcing and these are some of the  partners of closures together pitch new  Bank Juxt who was speaking at the  conference mitosis --n ed goji and  funding circle these are some of the  companies that have worked with us its  closures together to help us so I'd  really like to say thanks to them so  what can you do the first thing is  consider how you talk about your open  source dependencies in your companies  when you're talking about projects think  about you know think of that there's  people on the other end of these  projects and you know what does that  mean how how should we talk about that  how should we  think about those people next thing is  talking to your work about supporting  the open source dependencies you use  that might be through putting time into  making a patch or fixing an issue or  triaging a bunch of the things that  people have posted where it's maybe not  even clear what they're trying to say  and or giving money to the project and  lastly think critically about the  messages and the mediums that you build  if you're building software for  developers or open source developers  then think about what are the patterns  of interaction that we're designing for  is this what we want to be happening and  I have a few acknowledgments firstly the  strange loop I really appreciate them  giving me their chance to speak about  this and talk at this conference and all  of the closures together members we have  hundreds of developer members and tens  of company members who have all given  money to help us you know create this  open-source work and taught us a lot  about funding open-source it also like  to thank Ruby together an Andre Arco in  particular Ruby together came before us  and we copied you know lots of what they  did and Andre Arco in particular has  given us a lot of time to help us you  know learn from what they've done I'd  like to think the software freedom  Conservancy they are our nonprofit  parents and so they provide a lot of the  financial and accounting and legal  support that you need when you are  distributing money all around the world  this is something which is not something  that I had really thought about until we  encountered that and it's an incredible  amount of work and you can often go very  underappreciated so yeah I really  appreciate them I'd also like to say  thanks to my wife I've spend a lot of  time maintaining open source software  and doing projects and there has  sometimes come at the cost of family  life and yeah she's been great about  giving me that time and looking after  the kids you know I'm here at this  conference you know and she's at home  with the kids and I feel you know pretty  conflicted about there and so let's say  thanks to to my wife and also to all of  the partners of open-source maintainer  z-- that have  given them time to do this work that we  have all benefited from I know many  people you know do this work in their  spare time and their free hours and so  I'd like to say thanks to everyone who  has done that  thank you  [Applause]
 

